Hospital Operations, Cost & Patient Flow Intelligence

Statistical Investigation & Insight Discovery (Track B: Data Science)Programme: AnalystLab Africa Experience Lab Internship

Project: Week 3 — Healthcare Analytics and AI Implementation

Track: Track B: Data Science

Organization Reviewed: Massachusetts General Hospital

Report File: hospital_operations_statistical_report.pdf

Analysis Notebook: hospital_operations_statistical_analysis.ipynb1. Executive SummaryHealthcare administrators need reliable visibility into patient visit volumes, length of stay, billed costs, and insurance coverage to set budgets and staff clinical departments. This study examined eleven years of historical records from Massachusetts General Hospital (January 2011–February 2022). The data covered 27,891 patient encounters, 47,701 clinical procedures, 974 unique patients, and 10 insurance payers.Exploratory analysis and non-parametric statistical tests in Python showed that routine ambulatory visits account for nearly half of all traffic (44.95%). Inpatient admissions, however, carry the highest median cost ($2,355.59 per visit) and the longest stays (median 24 hours). Longer visits are moderately associated with higher claim costs. Insurance reimbursement also varies sharply across payers; nearly one-third of encounters (31.58%) are recorded as uninsured with zero coverage.2. Business Problem & Research QuestionsThe Operational Challenge

Hospital operations and finance teams need a clear view of how different visit types consume resources and how much of those costs are recovered through insurance. Without cost and length-of-stay breakdowns by care setting, leadership risks under-allocating resources to high-intensity departments or missing large uncompensated care gaps.Research Questions
Primary: How do recorded encounter costs (TOTAL_CLAIM_COST and BASE_ENCOUNTER_COST) and insurance coverage (PAYER_COVERAGE) vary across hospital encounter classes?
Supporting 1: Are longer encounter durations associated with higher total claim costs?
Supporting 2: How do claim costs and insurance coverage differ across insurance payers and frequently performed procedures?
Intended Audience & Decisions Supported

This report is intended for hospital operations managers, healthcare financial analysts, and revenue-cycle teams. The findings support three practical decisions:
Aligning staffing and bed capacity between high-volume outpatient clinics and high-cost inpatient units.
Identifying care settings and payer groups with large out-of-pocket or uncompensated billing gaps.
Establishing baseline cost benchmarks for departmental budgeting.
3. Dataset Inspection & Data PreparationAll five core datasets were audited in Python before any statistical tests. No exact duplicate rows were found, and all timestamps were clean with no negative visit durations.Dataset Summary








































File NameRecordsColumns UsedKey Findings & Data Hygiene Decisionsencounters.csv27,89114Covers 2 Jan 2011 to 5 Feb 2022 (February 2022 is a partial month). REASONCODE and REASONDESCRIPTION are blank in 19,541 routine visits; these rows were retained.procedures.csv47,7019Links to encounters via ENCOUNTER. REASONCODE is blank in 36,945 routine/preventive procedures and was retained.patients.csv97417Directly identifying fields (FIRST, LAST, ADDRESS) were removed on loading to protect privacy. DEATHDATE is blank for the 820 living patients.payers.csv107Lists 9 insurance plans plus NO_INSURANCE (blank address and phone fields are legitimate).organizations.csv18Identifies Massachusetts General Hospital in Boston, MA.Relational Structure & Feature Engineering

Encounters form the central table, linking many-to-one to patients, payers, and organizations, and one-to-many to procedures.Calculated fields:
Exact visit duration in hours from START and STOP timestamps (duration_hours)
Total procedures per visit (procedure_count)
Uncompensated coverage gap (TOTAL_CLAIM_COST – PAYER_COVERAGE)
4. Success CriteriaThe analysis was evaluated against four standards:
Accurate reconciliation: All grouped counts, medians, and averages were cross-checked against raw table totals with no discrepancies.
Method alignment: Distribution skewness was checked both visually and numerically before non-parametric tests were selected.
Privacy & reproducibility: The Jupyter notebook runs cleanly from top to bottom, exports high-resolution charts automatically, and contains no patient names or street addresses.
Honest interpretation: Results distinguish statistical significance from practical impact and avoid claims of clinical causation.
5. Statistical Methodology & HypothesesHospital cost and length-of-stay data are heavily right-skewed. A small number of complex inpatient stays pull averages far above typical visit costs. Because the distributions are non-normal, non-parametric tests that compare medians and rank orders were used. All tests used a significance level of 0.05.Hypothesis 1 – Costs across care settings
Null: Median TOTAL_CLAIM_COST is the same across all six encounter classes.
Alternative: At least one encounter class has a significantly different median TOTAL_CLAIM_COST.
Test: Kruskal-Wallis H-test.
Hypothesis 2 – Visit duration vs. claim cost
Null: No monotonic relationship exists between encounter duration (hours) and TOTAL_CLAIM_COST.
Alternative: Longer durations are monotonically associated with higher TOTAL_CLAIM_COST.
Test: Spearman rank correlation.
Hypothesis 3 – Insurance coverage across payers
Null: Median PAYER_COVERAGE does not differ across insurance payers.
Alternative: At least one payer shows significantly different recorded coverage.
Test: Kruskal-Wallis H-test.
6. Exploratory Findings & Statistical Results6.1 Encounter Volume & Cost by Care SettingHospital traffic is concentrated in outpatient settings; costs peak in inpatient care.




























































