[Insert Figure 2: Visualisations/02_metric_by_encounter_class.png]6.2 Visit Duration & Claim Cost RelationshipStatistical result (Hypothesis 2): Spearman ρ = 0.3379, p < 0.0001. A statistically significant, moderate positive association exists between visit length and total claim cost.Practical meaning: Longer stays, especially inpatient admissions (average 36.84 hours), generally cost more. The moderate correlation (0.34) shows that length of stay is not the only cost driver; brief outpatient or ambulatory visits that include specialized diagnostics or therapies can also generate substantial claims.[Insert Figure 3: Visualisations/03_duration_vs_metric.png]6.3 Payer Mix & Insurance Coverage GapsMedicare accounts for the largest share of visits (11,371 encounters; mean claim $2,167.55; mean coverage $1,689.89). NO_INSURANCE is the second-largest group (8,807 encounters, 31.58% of all visits) with an average claim of $5,593.20 and $0.00 recorded coverage. Public programs such as Medicaid (mean coverage $5,833.66) and Dual Eligible ($1,513.93) cover most billed charges. Several commercial plans (Humana, Aetna, UnitedHealthcare, Cigna Health, Anthem) show $0.00 median recorded coverage in the extract.Statistical result (Hypothesis 3): Kruskal-Wallis H = 22239.03, p < 0.0001. Recorded reimbursement differs substantially across payers.[Insert Figure 4: Visualisations/04_encounters_by_payer.png]6.4 Procedure Utilisation & Cost DriversAcross 47,701 recorded procedures, high-volume standardized assessments dominate:
Assessment using Morse Fall Scale (2,422 procedures; median base cost $431.00)
Assessment of anxiety (2,288; $431.00)
Medication Reconciliation (2,284; median $496.00)
Screening for drug abuse (1,484; $431.00)
Individual screening costs are modest, but high repetition creates steady baseline operational demand.[Insert Figure 5: Visualisations/05_top_procedures_frequency.png]7. Data Limitations & Methodological Caveats
Billed claims vs. real expenses: BASE_ENCOUNTER_COST and TOTAL_CLAIM_COST reflect recorded claim charges, not actual hospital operating costs (staff wages, utilities, supplies) or final cash collected after insurer adjustments.
Repeated patient visits: The 27,891 encounters belong to 974 unique patients. Encounter-level tests treat visits as separate operational events rather than independent individuals.
Single-facility scope: All encounters occurred at Massachusetts General Hospital; results may not generalize to smaller rural clinics or specialty hospitals.
Commercial payer recording: Several private insurers show $0.00 median coverage, which may reflect synthetic dataset rules or high-deductible plan structures rather than actual denials.
Association vs. causation: Observed links between care setting, visit length, and claim cost describe billing patterns only and do not establish clinical causation.
8. Recommendations1. Set length-of-stay and cost benchmarks for high-acuity units

Inpatient encounters have a median cost of $2,355.59 (more than 8× outpatient costs) and a median stay of 24 hours. Urgent care shows extreme high-cost outliers (mean $6,369.16 vs. median $142.58).

Action: Establish review triggers for inpatient stays exceeding 36 hours and audit high-cost urgent care claims to separate routine walk-ins from complex cases.

Limitation: True departmental margins require internal cost-accounting data.2. Expand front-desk coverage screening and financial counseling

Nearly one-third of encounters (8,807 visits, 31.58%) are recorded under NO_INSURANCE with an average claim of $5,593.20 and $0 coverage.

Action: Introduce proactive insurance verification and financial-assistance screening during ambulatory and outpatient registration so eligible patients can be connected earlier to Medicaid or subsidized programs.

Limitation: Actual bad-debt write-off figures are needed to estimate recoverable uncompensated care.3. Streamline workflows for high-frequency standardized screenings

Fall-risk scales, anxiety screenings, medication reconciliations, and substance screenings account for thousands of repeated procedures at fixed base rates ($431–$496).

Action: Bundle routine intake assessments into standardized digital pre-visit workflows to reduce clinic bottlenecks and claim fragmentation.9. Attribution & GovernanceData source: Healthcare Operations Dataset Pack (encounters.csv, patients.csv, procedures.csv, payers.csv, organizations.csv) supplied by AnalystLab Africa for Week 3 of the Experience Lab Internship Programme.Tools used: Python 3, Pandas, NumPy, SciPy, Matplotlib, Seaborn, Jupyter Notebook (VS Code), LibreOffice Writer.Privacy compliance: All patient names and street addresses were removed before analysis. All reported metrics are aggregated operational figures only.

